I applied for a DevOps role recently.

3+ years of experience required.
AWS / Azure / GCP.
ECS, EKS, Fargate.
CloudWatch. Grafana.
Terraform. Python. CI/CD.
The usual checklist.
Pretty much every tool was there.

What wasn’t there?
How you think about systems.
How you design for failure.
How you make something resilient instead of fragile.
How you make decisions that reduce cost without breaking things.
How security is designed and enforced from Day 1.

I got rejected.
No interview.
No conversation.
No feedback.
Just filtered out.
Because on paper I have 1 year of experience.
Here’s what that “1 year” looks like.

I started from zero in cloud.
Picked up Azure during a Microsoft CSP internship. Networking, pipelines, deployments,Terraform., AI foundry.
Then got the opportunity to work with two startups.
Both needed AWS infrastructure from scratch.
I had never worked on AWS before that.
Still said yes.
Ended up being the solo DevOps engineer for both.
At the same time.

Built production infrastructure.
Set up 18 CI/CD pipelines across repos and environments.
Reduced deployment time:
Frontend 45 minutes to 1 minute
Backend 90 minutes to 5 minutes
Optimized Docker image:
1.34 GB to 263 MB
Reduced infrastructure cost:
$104 to $0 for 5 months.
Handled production incidents independently at 2 AM.
And the infrastructure?
It held up.

I remember speaking with a senior engineer.
We were discussing production practices.
He said:
“Always create a non-root user in Docker.”
I told him I was already doing that in production.
With multi-stage builds, lightweight images, layer optimizations.

He paused and said:
“I can’t believe you’re not just aware of this you’ve already implemented it in production with less than a year of experience.”
Different teams. Same assumption until they hear the timeline.

So I keep coming back to this.
If someone can learn in months what used to take years is that a signal or a noise?

We talk about moving faster
Learn faster. Build faster. Ship faster.
But when someone actually does that they still get filtered out.
Because the tenure is small.
I’m not saying experience doesn’t matter.
But are we measuring experience or just time spent?
Because those aren’t the same anymore.

The best engineers I’ve seen don’t think in tools.
They think in trade-offs, failure scenarios, cost vs reliability, business impact.

Day 1 they focus on identity, secrets, and blast radius.
But hiring filters?
They still start with:
Years
Keywords
Checklists
Before anyone looks at:
What someone has built
What they’ve handled
How they think

Sometimes people who have already done the work
don’t even make it to the first conversation.
People say they hire for impact.
But still filter for tenure.
So I’ll ask this.

Should hiring still be based on years of experience?
Or should it focus on the problems solved and the results already delivered?

